            <ArticleTitle>Transfalciform greater omental herniation.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr-2017-219563</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2017-219563</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Internal herniae are rare, those involving the falciform ligament even rarer. To the best of our knowledge, there have been approximately 20 cases of herniae involving the falciform ligament previously reported. Of these cases, only one previously reported case involves herniation of the greater omentum through the falciform ligament. We present a second case of an adult man who presented with a 48-hour history of epigastric pain and was found on contrast multidetector CT to have a strangulated transfalciform greater omental hernia. The hernia was repaired laparoscopically and the patient recovered uneventfully.</AbstractText>
